ABSTRACT: With the growth of cyber threats across social media platforms, the requirement for automated, scalable, and context-aware threat detection has become increasingly crucial. Large Language Models (LLMs) such as BERT, GPT, and LLaMA, among others, have emerged as transformative tools in cyber threat intelligence (CTI), capable of handling unstructured and multilingual content popular in social media environments. This systematic review categorizes and analyzes the recent landscape of research focused on the application of Large Language Models (LLMs) for threat detection and mitigation within social media contexts. The review spans 28 peer-reviewed studies, evaluating methods that include supervised fine-tuning, prompt engineering, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), and multi-agent architectures. The findings reveal that LLMs significantly outperform Machine Learning (ML) models in precision, real-time adaptability, and contextual inference, with various models achieving F1 scores above 90% in multilingual threat classification tasks. Conversely, challenges persist in handling data imbalance, adversarial content, hallucinations, and the ethical implications of automated observation. This review identifies primary gaps in current research and presents a roadmap for future enhancement in LLM-driven CTI procedures aimed at securing social media platforms. © 2025 IEEE.
