TITLE: Securing the Physical-Digital Frontier: AI for Anomaly Detection in OT, IoT, and Critical Infrastructure
KEYWORDS: agentic AI; anomaly detection; critical infrastructure; defensive uplift; edge AI; industrial control systems; IoT security; LLM-based monitoring; operational technology; Anomaly detection; Industrial management; Information systems; Internet of things; Network security; Public works; Risk analysis; Risk assessment; Agentic AI; Anomaly detection; Defensive uplift; Edge AI; Industrial control systems; Internet-of-thing security; Language model; Large language model-based monitoring; Model-based monitoring; Operational technologies; Critical infrastructures
ABSTRACT: Operational Technology (OT) networks, heterogeneous Internet-of-Things (IoT) deployments, and the information-technology systems that increasingly converge with them constitute a physical-digital frontier whose security properties are markedly different from those of conventional enterprise IT. Legacy industrial protocols, decade-long device lifecycles, and stringent latency constraints rule out many of the controls that work well in cloud and corporate environments, while the consequence of compromise extends from data loss to physical harm. This paper reviews the use of artificial intelligence specifically agentic, large language model (LLM)-augmented systems-for anomaly detection across this frontier. We argue that the salient contribution of AI is not autonomy but acceleration: AI compresses the time between an anomaly occurring and a validated, actionable alert, raising the defender's operational uplift ratio without removing the analyst from the loop. We separate cognitive support (interpreting noisy multi-source telemetry) from execution support (automated isolation of compromised nodes), formalize uplift in the defensive context, present a comparative analysis of detection requirements across OT, IoT, and IT layers, and discuss the symmetric risk that attackers will use the same techniques to evade detection. We close with operational recommendations for critical-infrastructure operators, emphasizing workflow design over flawless autonomy. © 2026 IEEE.
